WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
Where latency comes from.

2
00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:13.059
For a single response, a good first model of wall-clock time is: time to first token (TTFT), plus output tokens divided by output speed in tokens per second.

3
00:00:13.339 --> 00:00:22.064
TTFT covers network time, queueing, reading your input, and, on reasoning models, the thinking that happens before the first visible word.

4
00:00:22.344 --> 00:00:25.821
Output speed is how fast the model generates once it starts.

5
00:00:26.101 --> 00:00:28.107
The second term usually dominates.

6
00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:33.784
Suppose a model has a TTFT of one second and generates 50 tokens per second.

7
00:00:34.064 --> 00:00:37.200
A 100-token answer takes about 3 seconds.

8
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.787
A 1,500-token answer takes about 31.

9
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:44.160
Nothing about the input changed; the answer got longer.

10
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:59.117
That is why the highest-leverage latency fixes are about output: ask for shorter answers, return structured fields instead of prose, lower the reasoning effort on routine requests, and pick a faster model for latency-critical paths.

11
00:00:59.397 --> 00:01:05.371
Streaming (lesson 2) does not shrink total time, but it makes TTFT the number users feel.

12
00:01:05.651 --> 00:01:10.088
Input length matters less than output length per token, but it is not free.

13
00:01:10.368 --> 00:01:16.363
Long prompts raise TTFT and cost, and prompt caching reduces both for repeated prefixes.

14
00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:30.701
Independent services such as Artificial Analysis measure TTFT and output speed across providers’ endpoints; its methodology averages recent measurements and counts reasoning tokens in output speed.

15
00:01:30.981 --> 00:01:40.197
Use such numbers to shortlist, then measure on your own prompts and from your own region, because latency depends on load, prompt shape, and time of day.
